 "voice_note": {
  "mp3": "assets/audio/st_lp_voicenote.mp3",
  "script": "Dumela titjhere. Kajeno ke letsatsi la pele la beke ya bone, mme sehlooho sa rona ke dijo tseo re di jang. Baithuti ba tla bua ka seo ba se jeleng hoseng, ba ithute modumo o motjha, ba bale mantswe dipaketeng tsa dijo, mme ba bale buka e ntjha ka dihlopha. Ntho e le nngwe eo ke kopang o e etse hantle kajeno ke ena: tlohela baithuti ba tswake dinoko ka bobona. Ha o bolela modumo wa m le modumo wa u, o se ke wa ba bolella noko. Ba tlohele ba fumane mu ka bobona. Modumo wa kajeno ke u, jwaloka kgomo ha e re muu. Bontsha baithuti hore molomo o monyane o chitja. Mantswe a bohlokwa kajeno ke mu, muu, mama, mme le mema. Bukeng ya sehlopha ba tla bala polelo e reng Mme o a mema. Ha ba thatafallwa, hosane qala hape ka ma le mu feela. Ke o lakaletsa thuto e monate!",
  "script_en": "Hello, teacher. Today is the first day of week four, and our theme is the food we eat. Learners will talk about what they ate this morning, learn a new sound, read words on food packets, and read a new book in groups. The one thing I ask you to get right today is this: let the learners blend the syllables themselves. When you say the sound m and the sound u, do not tell them the syllable. Let them find mu on their own. Today's sound is u, like the cow when it says muu. Show the learners that the lips are small and round. The important words today are mu, muu, mama, mme and mema. In the group book they will read the sentence Mme o a mema, mother invites us. If they struggle, start again tomorrow with ma and mu only. I wish you a lovely lesson!",
